{"duration_ms":0,"id":24,"kind":"llm","model":"claude-opus-5","name":"claude-code.turn.15","provider":"anthropic","response":{"content":[{"text":"**Fixed** — one character, in `stats.py:3`.\n\n```python\n-    return sum(values) // len(values)\n+    return sum(values) / len(values)\n```\n\n`mean` was using `//` (floor division). Whenever the sum of the samples was smaller than the number of samples — exactly the case when every sample is a fraction of a second — the result floored to `0`. It also silently truncated in the ordinary case: `[120, 340, 90]` reported `183` instead of `183.33`.\n\nVerified: sub-second samples now average `0.55` instead of `0`, and a single sample of `0.5` returns `0.5` instead of `0`.\n\nOne thing worth flagging: `mean` is shared, so this also changes `billing.average_invoice`, which was truncating average invoice values to whole currency units (`[10, 15]` → `12` before, `12.5` now). That's a fix rather than a regression given the docstring says \"arithmetic mean,\" but if anything downstream of billing assumes an `int` return, it will now see a `float`. There are no tests in the repo to confirm either way — I didn't add rounding or formatting anywhere, since the display layer wasn't part of what you asked about.","type":"text"}],"id":"msg_011CeuMF2FEJqocRhzaTtg9G","model":"claude-opus-5","role":"assistant","stop_reason":null,"usage":{"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":295},"cache_creation_input_tokens":295,"cache_read_input_tokens":19446,"inference_geo":"not_available","input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard"}},"start_ms":0,"usage":{"cache_read":19446,"cache_write_5m":295,"input":2,"output":1}}
